<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualWorldPart> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text appearing on signs, books, screens, and other objects within the meme world"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#assertion_about_01295> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#hate> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#consilientWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a man dressed in clerical attire (red robe and white hat) sitting alone in a row of empty chairs, with overlaid text that reads: 'men wearing dresses and molesting our children?' and 'the word you're looking for is clergy, not transgender.' The overall intent is to use the visual of a clergyman juxtaposed with the text to imply that all clergy are pedophiles or molesters, and to mock or misdirect the term 'transgender' by suggesting it is a misnomer for 'clergy.' This meme is harmful and hateful as it targets religious figures (specifically clergy) by associating them with child molestation, which is a serious and false accusation. It also uses the term 'transgender' in a derogatory and misleading way, potentially contributing to transphobic sentiment by conflating gender identity with pedophilia. The meme is propagandistic in nature, aiming to influence public opinion against clergy and transgender individuals by creating a false equivalence and using fear-based rhetoric. The visual of the solitary clergyman in an empty hall reinforces a sense of isolation and guilt, amplifying the toxic message. The meme is also cyberbullying, as it attacks a specific group (clergy) based on their identity and role, and it is abusive in its implication of widespread criminal behavior. The meme's intent is to provoke outrage and reinforce negative stereotypes about religious institutions and transgender people, potentially causing harm to both groups by stigmatizing them and encouraging prejudice."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#metaphoricallyMapsTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#causallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "causally related to"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#pointingAdjacentTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subPropertyOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semanticallyAdjacentTo> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#rhythmicallyIntegratedWith> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "33"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
_:n06cbaefab1b841fda34e595d4e11518eb109 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:n06cbaefab1b841fda34e595d4e11518eb110 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Background Knowledge elements from the image. Background Knowledge includes: cultural references, historical context, social knowledge, domain-specific information, contextual cues, and background information. For each knowledge element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'cultural_reference', 'historical_context'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the cultural reference', 'the historical context'), 3) A brief description of the knowledge or context required to understand the element. Focus on information that viewers need to know to fully comprehend the meme. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"cultural_reference\", \"label\": \"the cultural reference\", \"description\": \"Reference to the 'This is fine' dog meme, requiring knowledge of internet culture to understand the irony\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Metadata> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Metadata elements from the image. Metadata includes: technical information, contextual descriptions, descriptive attributes, metadata properties, and informational elements. For each metadata element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'image_format', 'creation_context'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the image format', 'the creation context'), 3) A brief description of the metadata information. Focus on descriptive and technical information about the meme itself. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"image_format\", \"label\": \"the image format\", \"description\": \"Digital image format with high contrast and clear visual elements typical of internet memes\"}"@en .
