<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dramaticallyIronicalTo> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "14"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#verballyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
_:n06cbaefab1b841fda34e595d4e11518eb83 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is implicitly projected onto the visual of the clergyman, who is being accused by the text 'men wearing dresses and molesting our children?'. The meme frames the user as someone who is being misled or is being asked to make a false association, as the text then says 'the word you're looking for is clergy, not transgender'. This implies the user is being directed to see the clergyman as the correct target of the accusation, rather than transgender individuals. The projection is onto the visual material (clergyman) and the textual material (top_text) which together create the accusation and the misdirection."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#visuallyCompositionCompliantWith> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
